---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Setup | ThreadSherpa"
  description="Run ThreadSherpa yourself: create a Discord bot, connect MongoDB, choose Ollama or Paper Instruments, and connect one support forum."
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap page-hero__inner">
      <p class="kicker">Setup guide</p>
      <h1>Start with one support channel.</h1>
      <p class="page-hero__lede">
        ThreadSherpa runs on your machine or server. You create the Discord application, connect a
        database, choose a model, and decide where the bot can help. No hosted signup, no shared
        bot account.
      </p>
      <div class="page-hero__actions">
        <a class="btn btn--primary" href="#connect-forum">Connect a forum</a>
        <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/security/">Read the security boundaries</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div class="page-body">
    <div class="wrap guide-layout">
      <aside class="guide-toc" aria-label="On this page">
        <h2>On this page</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><a href="#before">Before you start</a></li>
          <li><a href="#discord">Create the bot</a></li>
          <li><a href="#storage">Storage and models</a></li>
          <li><a href="#env">Configuration</a></li>
          <li><a href="#check">Check and start</a></li>
          <li><a href="#connect-forum">Connect a forum</a></li>
          <li><a href="#save">Save the first answer</a></li>
          <li><a href="#trouble">If something blocks you</a></li>
        </ol>
        <p class="guide-toc__back"><a href="/#how-it-works">Back to the overview</a></p>
      </aside>

      <article class="prose">
        <section id="before">
          <h2>Before you start</h2>
          <p>
            You need a project checkout, Node.js 22 or newer, a Discord account, and a database. A
            local model also needs Ollama and enough memory to run it. Hosted model providers can
            be used instead, but they receive the questions and evidence used for replies.
          </p>

          <div class="notice">
            <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" aria-hidden="true">
              <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="6.4" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.3"></circle>
              <path d="M8 4.6v4.2" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round"></path>
              <circle cx="8" cy="11.6" r="0.9" fill="currentColor"></circle>
            </svg>
            <p>
              <strong>Use your ThreadSherpa checkout.</strong>
              Repository access is currently required. The bot uses Node.js 22 or newer;
              building this website needs Node.js 22.23.4 or newer.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Use a dedicated test server first. Do not import another community's messages,
            screenshots, or policies as a knowledge base. The bot reads new events in channels you
            connect; it does not backfill historical posts.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="discord">
          <h2>1. Create the Discord bot</h2>
          <ol>
            <li>
              Open the
              <a class="external" href="https://discord.com/developers/applications" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Discord Developer Portal</a>
              and create an application. Name it ThreadSherpa.
            </li>
            <li>
              Open <strong>Bot</strong>, reset or copy the bot token, and keep it private. The token
              goes in <code>DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN</code>. A reset invalidates the previous token.
            </li>
            <li>
              On the Bot page, enable <strong>Message Content Intent</strong>. ThreadSherpa does not
              request Server Members Intent or Presence Intent.
            </li>
            <li>
              Open <strong>Installation</strong> and enable <strong>Guild Install</strong>. Invite
              the bot with the <code>bot</code> and <code>applications.commands</code> scopes. It
              does not need Administrator.
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            The bot needs View Channels, Read Message History, Send Messages, Send Messages in
            Threads (or Send Messages in Posts), and Embed Links in the support channel. Thread
            permissions are inherited from the parent forum, but channel overrides can still block
            it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Reference:
            <a class="external" href="https://docs.discord.com/developers/quick-start/getting-started" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Discord's bot setup guide</a>
            and
            <a class="external" href="https://discord.com/developers/docs/topics/permissions" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Discord permissions</a>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="storage">
          <h2>2. Choose storage and a model</h2>
          <h3>MongoDB</h3>
          <p>
            Create a free MongoDB Atlas cluster, a database user, and a network access entry for
            the machine running the bot. Copy the driver connection string. It looks like
            <code>mongodb+srv://…</code> and includes the database user and password. Password
            characters reserved in URLs must be percent-encoded.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <a class="external" href="https://cloud.mongodb.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MongoDB Atlas</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a class="external" href="https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/tutorial/deploy-free-tier-cluster/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Create a free cluster</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a class="external" href="https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/security-add-mongodb-users/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Add a database user</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a class="external" href="https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/security/ip-access-list/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Add an IP access entry</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a class="external" href="https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/driver-connection/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Get the driver connection string</a>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <h3>Local inference with Ollama</h3>
          <p>
            Install <a class="external" href="https://ollama.com/download" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ollama</a>
            and pull the reply model and embedding model named in the project's environment
            template. Keep Ollama running while the bot is running. Local inference keeps the
            synthesis request on your machine; Discord and the database still use the network.
          </p>

          <h3>Paper Instruments</h3>
          <p>
            Paper is optional. It uses server-side API credit for synthesis, while local embeddings
            still run through Ollama. Add its key only on the machine running the bot. Do not put a
            provider key in browser code, a screenshot, or a commit.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="env">
          <h2>3. Configure the environment</h2>
          <p>
            Create <code>.env</code> from the project's <code>.env.example</code>. Keep it out of
            version control. These are the variable names you will see; values are yours and should
            never be posted publicly.
          </p>

          <div class="prose-table">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>DISCORD_BOT_TOKEN</code></td><td>The bot token from the Discord Developer Portal.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>MONGODB_URI</code></td><td>The Atlas driver connection string.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>MONGODB_DB_NAME</code></td><td>Optional database name. The default is <code>threadsherpa</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>MODEL_PROVIDER</code></td><td><code>ollama</code> or <code>paper</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>PAPER_INSTRUMENTS_API_KEY</code></td><td>Only for Paper mode. Optional otherwise.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>DISCORD_DEV_GUILD_ID</code></td><td>Optional test-server ID for immediate command registration.</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            The project's setup checker creates <code>.env</code> only when it does not exist. It
            never overwrites existing keys. It checks access without posting Discord messages or
            requesting paid generation.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="check">
          <h2>4. Check the configuration and start</h2>
          <div class="code-block" data-copy-scope>
            <div class="code-block__bar">
              <span class="code-block__label">Terminal</span>
              <button class="copy-btn" type="button" data-copy="npm ci
npm run setup
npm run dev">Copy commands</button>
            </div>
            <pre><code>npm ci
npm run setup
npm run dev</code></pre>
            <p class="copy-status" role="status" data-copy-status></p>
          </div>
          <p>
            Run the checker after each configuration change. You want Discord, MongoDB, and the
            chosen models to report OK. The checker prints a ready-to-use invite URL when the
            Discord token works. Open it, choose your test server, and authorize the bot. For a
            production process, use <code>npm run build</code> then <code>npm start</code>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="connect-forum">
          <h2>5. Connect the support forum</h2>
          <p>
            A forum's top-level view has no normal message composer, so do not run setup there.
            Run the command from a regular text channel, such as <code>#bot-commands</code>, as a
            server administrator:
          </p>
          <div class="code-block" data-copy-scope>
            <div class="code-block__bar">
              <span class="code-block__label">In Discord</span>
              <button class="copy-btn" type="button" data-copy="/setup channel:#your-forum autonomy_mode:auto">Copy command</button>
            </div>
            <pre><code>/setup channel:#your-forum autonomy_mode:auto</code></pre>
            <p class="copy-status" role="status" data-copy-status></p>
          </div>
          <p>
            Select your actual support forum in the command's <strong>channel</strong> option. This
            works before the forum has any posts, and you do not repeat it for every question.
            Commands run in the text channel; the bot connects the selected forum.
          </p>
          <p>
            If you already have a forum post, you can alternatively run
            <code>/setup autonomy_mode:auto</code> inside that post on first setup; the bot selects
            its parent forum. For an ordinary text support channel, run
            <code>/setup autonomy_mode:auto</code> directly in it, or select it with the
            <strong>channel</strong> option.
          </p>
          <p>
            Automatic replies apply to new posts in the selected forum. Unknown questions wait
            quietly in <code>/queue</code>. To stop monitoring a forum, run
            <code>/setup remove_channel:#your-forum</code> from a regular text channel.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="save">
          <h2>6. Save the first answer</h2>
          <ol>
            <li>Create a synthetic question in a support post.</li>
            <li>
              Reply as a moderator, then right-click your answer, open <strong>Apps</strong>, and
              choose <strong>Save this answer</strong>. You will see <strong>Saved.</strong> or
              <strong>Already saved.</strong>
            </li>
            <li>
              Ask a new question in different words. When the saved facts support it, ThreadSherpa
              writes a short reply and links the original answer.
            </li>
            <li>
              Check <code>/queue</code> for questions that were not answered. Use
              <code>/answers</code> to edit or remove saved guidance, and <code>/teach</code> when
              there is no existing answer message to save.
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            A clear “that worked” confirmation closes a case without another bot message. A “that
            didn't work” reply queues the attempted advice for review. Saving knowledge does not
            post into older questions.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section id="trouble">
          <h2>If something blocks you</h2>
          <div class="prose-table">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr><th>What you see</th><th>What to check</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <td>Discord token rejected</td>
                  <td>Use the Bot Token, not the client secret or application ID. If you reset it, update <code>.env</code>.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Bot cannot read posts</td>
                  <td>Enable Message Content Intent on the Bot page, then restart the bot.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>MongoDB cannot connect</td>
                  <td>Check the database user, password encoding, and the current IP entry in Atlas.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Slash commands are missing</td>
                  <td>Invite the bot with the URL from the checker and restart it. For multiple servers, set <code>DISCORD_DEV_GUILD_ID</code> for immediate test-server registration.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>No reply in a forum post</td>
                  <td>Run <code>/setup channel:#your-forum autonomy_mode:auto</code> from a text channel. Unknown questions are intentionally quiet; check <code>/queue</code> and the forum permissions.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                  <td>Ollama is missing</td>
                  <td>Open Ollama, pull the models named in the environment template, and run the checker again.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            Read the <a href="/security/">security boundaries</a> before using real community data.
            For operational limits and data handling, the project's security notes are the
            authority.
          </p>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
